    "Tether technique: put your foot on the leash so your puppy can't reach your hand. Lure toward the ground but keep your hand just out of reach (~5 cm gap). They'll eventually slide into a down to get closer.": {
      "comment": "Technique to use with the \"Down\" skill to help your puppy learn to lie down.",
      "isCommentAutoGenerated": true
    },
    "Thank your pup for alerting you, then redirect with a 'quiet' cue or toy.": {},
    "Thanks for sharing!": {},
    "That means your dog already knows how to learn. They're ready. Willing. Eager.": {
      "comment": "Paragraph 3 of the \"Welcome\" section in the Foundations module.",
      "isCommentAutoGenerated": true
    },
    "That the command is \"sit-sit-SIT-with-anger\"": {
      "comment": "Explanation of why the dog is learning the command \"sit\" when the trainer is shouting it.",
      "isCommentAutoGenerated": true
    },
    "That they should sit faster": {
      "comment": "Option A in the \"The Word Trap\" quiz on the Training.Foundations.HowDogsLearn page.",
      "isCommentAutoGenerated": true
    },
    "That you're frustrated (but nothing about sitting)": {
      "comment": "Explanation of why the correct answer to the \"The Word Trap\" quiz question is the third option.",
      "isCommentAutoGenerated": true
    },
    "That's completely fine.": {
      "comment": "Highlight text in the \"It's Harder for You\" section of the Foundations module.",
      "isCommentAutoGenerated": true
    },
    "That's it. The rest is practice.": {
      "comment": "Closing text in the \"The Big Secret\" section of the \"Getting Started\" module in the Training Foundations extension.",
      "isCommentAutoGenerated": true
    },
    "That's right!": {},
    "The '5-minute rule' — puppies can walk 5 minutes per month of age per session.": {},
    "The 1-minute rule": {},
    "The 3 D's": {
      "comment": "Title of a section in the \"Taking It Further\" module of the Training Foundations.",
      "isCommentAutoGenerated": true
    },
    "The Adolescent Regression": {
      "comment": "Title of a section in the \"Taking It Further\" module of the Foundations extension of the Training enum in Strings+Training.swift.",
      "isCommentAutoGenerated": true
    },
    "The Beginning": {},
    "The Big Secret": {
      "comment": "Title of a section in the Foundations module that discusses the \"Big Secret\" of training.",
      "isCommentAutoGenerated": true
    },
    "The Goal: Calm Neutrality": {},
    "The Step-Back Principle": {
      "comment": "Tip for training, emphasizing the importance of returning to the initial steps if a behavior doesn't improve in a new environment.",
      "isCommentAutoGenerated": true
    },
    "The Step-Back Rule": {
      "comment": "Title of a section in the Training module about the Step-Back Rule.",
      "isCommentAutoGenerated": true
    },
    "The Word Trap": {
      "comment": "Title of a quiz within the Foundations.HowDogsLearn section.",
      "isCommentAutoGenerated": true
    },
    "The app learns from patterns and predicts when your puppy needs to pee.": {},
    "The click ends the exercise": {
      "comment": "Explanation of why clicking at the exact moment of correct behavior is important.",
      "isCommentAutoGenerated": true,
      "localizations": {
        "nl": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "De klik beëindigt de oefening"
          }
        }
      }
    },
    "The click marks the exact moment of correct behavior": {
      "localizations": {
        "nl": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "De klik markeert het exacte moment van correct gedrag"
          }
        }
      }
    },
    "The click marks the exact moment of correct behavior. Always reward within 1-2 seconds. If you click by accident, still give a treat — maintaining the association is paramount.": {
      "comment": "Explanation of the timing aspect of the training principles.",
      "isCommentAutoGenerated": true
    },
    "The click marks the exact moment of correct behavior. It signals 'well done, you can relax now.' Don't expect the dog to hold position after clicking. Every click MUST be followed by a treat — clicking without treating destroys the association.": {
      "comment": "Explanation of the importance of clicking at the exact moment of correct behavior in training.",
      "isCommentAutoGenerated": true
    },
    "The click signals 'well done, you can relax now'. Don't expect the dog to hold position after clicking.": {},
    "The clicker is a marker signal that tells your puppy exactly when they did something right. It bridges the gap between the behavior and the reward. You can use a clicker OR a marker word like 'yes' — but pick one, not both.": {
      "comment": "Description of the clicker skill, including its use as a reward and as a recall tool.",
      "isCommentAutoGenerated": true
    },
    "The clicker is not permanent. Once a behavior is solid across many environments, you can phase it out. But variable reward continues for life.": {
      "comment": "Tip related to the clicker skill, explaining that it's not a permanent solution and that the association can be broken over time.",
      "isCommentAutoGenerated": true
    },
    "The crate gives you a timeout option and ensures your pup gets enough rest.": {},
    "The crate should always be positive. Never use it as punishment or your pup will resist going in.": {},
    "The data could not be read. Try restarting the app.": {},
    "The dog figures it out themselves": {
      "comment": "Explanation of how a dog learns behaviors through operant conditioning.",
      "isCommentAutoGenerated": true
    },
    "The first 16 weeks are critical. Track exposures to people, animals, sounds, and environments to raise a confident dog.": {},
    "The first weeks with a puppy are the most important. Otis helps you track patterns and build good habits.": {},
    "The goal is not to challenge your dog. The goal is to set them up for wins.": {
      "comment": "Goal of the \"Step-Back Rule\" in the Training Foundations module.",
      "isCommentAutoGenerated": true
    },
    "The goal: walk past a playground without breaking stride.": {},
    "The goal: your dog walks beside you, looking up at your face, on a loose leash. This is classical conditioning — you actively guide with food. The final test: 10 meters with eye contact and hand behind your back.": {
      "comment": "Description of the 'Loose Leash Walking' skill.",
      "isCommentAutoGenerated": true
    },
    "The golden rule: Only work on ONE at a time.": {
      "comment": "Explanation of the golden rule for training.",
      "isCommentAutoGenerated": true
    },
    "The good news? This language is simple. There are just a few rules:": {
      "comment": "Paragraph 3 in the \"The Big Secret\" section of the \"Welcome\" module in the Training Foundations extension.",
      "isCommentAutoGenerated": true
    },
    "The hand signal is introduced immediately — the lure IS the signal": {
      "comment": "Explanation of how to use a hand signal in training, emphasizing that the lure itself is the signal to click.",
      "isCommentAutoGenerated": true
    },
    "The instant your puppy makes eye contact — click and treat. Even a split-second glance at your face counts.": {
      "comment": "How-to step for the Watch Me skill: rewarding your puppy when they make eye contact.",
      "isCommentAutoGenerated": true
    },
    "The journey begins": {},
    "The magic word works because it's never 'polluted' by everyday use. Your dog's name and 'come' have been said thousands of times with no reward — they've learned to tune them out": {
      "comment": "Tip for the \"Come\" skill, emphasizing the importance of using the magic word sparingly and without everyday distractions.",
      "isCommentAutoGenerated": true
    },
    "The moment they sit or lie down → click → turn around → walk back → reward. Toss a treat behind them so they can sniff briefly.": {
      "comment": "How-to step for the \"Passive Stay\" method of training a dog to stay.",
      "isCommentAutoGenerated": true
    },
    "The name will always be imported.": {},
    "The over-socialization trap": {},
    "The puppy is exposed to something and nothing bad happens. She observes from a comfortable distance, maybe gets a treat for being calm, and you move on. That's the positive experience — the absence of stress.": {},
    "The rotation: with arm stretched to the right, rotate your LEFT shoulder inward. Your hand stays in exactly the same place — the dog doesn't notice any change. Practice: put your hand against a wall (hand can't move), then rotate your body.": {
      "comment": "Description of a step in the \"Loose Leash Walking\" training, focusing on the dog's body rotation.",
      "isCommentAutoGenerated": true
    },
    "The routine is taking shape.": {},
    "The shaded area shows healthy weight range for your puppy's breed size. Regular weigh-ins help spot growth issues early.": {},
    "The solution?": {
      "comment": "Title of a section in the \"Taking It Further\" module of the Training Foundations extension.",
      "isCommentAutoGenerated": true
    },
    "The tether prevents frustration from pushing forward": {
      "extractionState": "stale",
      "localizations": {
        "nl": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "De lijn voorkomt frustratie door naar voren duwen"
          }
        }
      }
    },
    "The truth:": {
      "comment": "Explanation of a concept that is not immediately obvious.",
      "isCommentAutoGenerated": true
    },
    "The well-known '5-minute rule' — adjust based on your puppy's energy level.": {
      "comment": "Description of the '5-minute rule' for adjusting exercise minutes based on a puppy's energy level.",
      "isCommentAutoGenerated": true
    },
    "The well-known '5-minute rule' — puppies can walk for a maximum of 5 minutes per month of age per session. Adjust based on your puppy's energy level.": {},
    "The word should be short (1-2 syllables), easy to say with excitement, and completely unique to recall. Never use it casually. This word = jackpot treat, always.": {
      "comment": "How-to step for choosing a magic word in the \"Magic Recall\" skill.",
      "isCommentAutoGenerated": true
    },
    "Their brain is rewiring. Hormones are surging. Independence is asserting itself. It's the dog equivalent of a teenager rolling their eyes at you.": {
      "comment": "Explanation of what happens to a dog's brain during adolescence.",
      "isCommentAutoGenerated": true
    },
    "Theme": {},
    "Then": {},
    "Then vs Now": {},
    "There are two approaches to training, and you've experienced both:": {
      "comment": "Explanation of the two training approaches.",
      "isCommentAutoGenerated": true
    },
    "These settings control which reminders and suggestions you see in the app and as notifications.": {},
    "These skills need attention": {},
    "They learn to settle and self-soothe instead of getting overtired and hyper": {},
    "They think training is about teaching the dog. But your dog already knows how to learn — they've been bred for this for 15,000 years.": {
      "comment": "Explanation of the misconception that training is about teaching the dog, rather than about teaching the trainer.",
      "isCommentAutoGenerated": true
    },
    "They'll ignore recalls. Blow off sits. Act like they've never heard their name.": {
      "comment": "Explanation of what can happen to a dog's memory during adolescence.",
      "isCommentAutoGenerated": true
    },
